+Forge 0.21 `behaviour.diyPoolOverlap` is per pack: `report` leaves the pool unchanged; `strip` removes only
+the declaring pack's chess-source operator identities from `backups.diy.ownedPool`, retaining their operator records.
+The importer writes a hashed `data/forge-diy-strip.json` patch document with a bounded `forgeDiyStrip` id list. Content
+composition collects those lists and removes matches after all selected records are expanded, so duplicate declarations
+are idempotent and another pack's undeclared operators are not removed. The importer reports each known overlap. This
+does not itself enable Forge custom match classes or server modules.
+
+A Forge server declaration containing exactly one standalone `registerServer` module whose only use is `matchClass`, without imports or writes, can be converted into a trusted room plugin. Import never runs its source; the existing trusted-mode, local-bind and lock-pin gates apply. Once selected, the verified module wraps the match class inside that content set's isolated host, in source-pack order; it is not activated at server startup. The wrapper receives its normalized `host.matchKinds()` data and a match `mods` snapshot of selected converted wrappers, not a complete Forge workshop inventory. `host.module` is preserved; `host.hash` names the checked plugin snapshot, and the original package directory is not exposed.
+
+A single standalone, read-only server module using only `boot`, `shutdown` and/or `healthz` can also become a trusted server plugin. Its callbacks run in the existing separate trusted process after the listener binds and before shutdown, respectively; boot receives a frozen, whitelisted configuration snapshot. Health callbacks run for each `/healthz` request, and bounded flat scalar results appear under `modHealth` by source pack ID. Other uses and combinations remain partial: custom match selection, additional seats, file I/O, routes, lanes and imported module dependencies require adaptation and are reported.
+
+Forge 0.21 `deploy`, `form` and `training` variants are composed by source pack ID, with the first declaration winning. `deploy: max` sets the deployment cap to 12. `form: elite` projects the elite record's combat fields onto the normal record while retaining normal identity, economy, bonds and garrison traits; owner-specific token variants follow the elite form. Projection is repeated after the optional tavern data overlay. Declarations participate in the content digest. The inspected Forge 0.21 runtime does not itself rewrite player loadouts for `training`; this adapter likewise uses that axis to select variant data and leaves loadouts unchanged.
+
+Forge `variants.json` can patch official records and mapped records from imported packs. Patches apply after record composition, only for the winning declaration of each axis; unknown targets or unsupported tables are reported, and unknown top-level fields reject the composition. Forge's keyed talent-list merge is retained. A target from another pack is noted in the import report and resolved when both packs are selected; if it remains unavailable, composition reports a warning. Source-to-target mappings are hashed and checked against the contributing plugin's record ownership. Packs imported before this mapping support need to be re-imported to provide cross-pack identities.
+
+When a Forge Spine explicitly declares `anims`, the source mapping is not copied; the lock infers animation roles from the skeleton. The import report marks this as partial and names the affected model because inferred roles can differ from the pack's choices.
+
+Imported Forge behaviour declarations also resolve conflicts by source pack ID. Native plugin rules continue to reject conflicting declarations; a Forge behaviour conflicting with a native rule is not silently selected.
